---
layout: layouts/tool.njk
toolSlug: html-formatter
title: "HTML Formatter & Beautifier Online"
description: "Format messy HTML with Prettier: clean indentation, inline CSS and JavaScript formatted too, parse errors with line numbers, and a careful minify mode."
permalink: /tools/html-formatter/
h1: "HTML Formatter"
quickAnswer: "Paste your HTML and press Format. Prettier puts each block element on its own line, indents nested elements by 2 spaces (or 4, or tabs), formats the CSS inside style tags and the JavaScript inside script tags, and keeps inline elements such as span and a on the same line when it can. If the markup cannot be parsed, for example a closing tag that does not match, you get the line and column instead."
formula:
  title: "How the HTML Is Formatted"
  expression: "Parse HTML → tree of elements → print with indentation and line width, keeping whitespace where the browser would render it"
  explanation: "The page uses Prettier's HTML printer, loaded from this site the first time you format. It parses the markup into a tree, then prints block elements (div, p, ul, section …) on their own lines with one level of indentation per nesting level, and breaks long lines at the print width you choose. Inline elements (span, a, strong, em …) are whitespace-sensitive: a line break between them shows up as a space on the page, so by default Prettier only adds breaks where the CSS display value says it is safe. CSS inside <style> is formatted with Prettier's CSS printer and JavaScript inside <script> with its JavaScript printer. Elements that are never closed are closed by the parser and reported as warnings with their line number."
  variables:
    - name: "Indent"
      description: "2 spaces (the most common choice for HTML), 4 spaces or tabs."
    - name: "Print width"
      description: "The line length Prettier aims for before wrapping attributes or content."
    - name: "Whitespace sensitivity"
      description: "css respects each element's default display; strict treats all whitespace as meaningful; ignore treats none of it as meaningful."
workedExamples:
  - title: "Nested elements on one line"
    before: |-
      <div><p>Hi<span>there</span></p></div>
    after: |-
      <div>
        <p>Hi<span>there</span></p>
      </div>
    steps:
      - "div and p are blocks, so each starts a new line and the p is indented"
      - "span is inline and touches the text, so adding a line break would add a visible space; it stays where it is"
  - title: "Inline CSS and JavaScript"
    before: |-
      <style>body{margin:0;color:#222}</style><script>function hi(name){return 'Hi '+name}</script>
    after: |-
      <style>
        body {
          margin: 0;
          color: #222;
        }
      </style>
      <script>
        function hi(name) {
          return "Hi " + name;
        }
      </script>
    steps:
      - "The style and script contents are handed to Prettier's CSS and JavaScript printers"
  - title: "A div inside a paragraph"
    before: |-
      <p>Intro
      <div>Block</div>
      </p>
    steps:
      - "A p cannot contain a div: the parser closes the p when the div starts"
      - "The final </p> then has no open p to close"
    result: "Line 3, column 1: Unexpected closing tag \"p\""
commonMistakes:
  - title: "Putting a block element inside a paragraph"
    description: "<code>&lt;p&gt;</code> may only contain phrasing content. A <code>&lt;div&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;ul&gt;</code> or <code>&lt;table&gt;</code> inside it ends the paragraph early, so styles and scripts aimed at the paragraph miss the rest of its content. Use a <code>&lt;div&gt;</code> as the wrapper instead."
  - title: "Leaving elements unclosed"
    description: "Browsers repair missing end tags, but not always where you expect, which can push later content inside the wrong element. Only a few elements, such as <code>&lt;li&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;p&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;td&gt;</code>, have optional end tags."
  - title: "Adding line breaks between inline elements"
    description: "<code>&lt;a&gt;Home&lt;/a&gt;&lt;a&gt;About&lt;/a&gt;</code> and the same links on two lines render differently: the line break becomes a space. That is why the formatter sometimes keeps inline elements together or uses the unusual <code>&gt;</code> placement at the start of the next line."
  - title: "Expecting minify to optimise everything"
    description: "Minify here only removes comments and the whitespace between tags. It does not shorten inline CSS or JavaScript, remove optional tags or quotes, or rewrite attributes. Use a build tool for production minification."
  - title: "Formatting templates as plain HTML"
    description: "Template syntax such as <code>{{ }}</code>, <code>{% %}</code> or JSX is not HTML. The formatter may move or break it. Format templates with a tool that understands that template language."
faqs:
  - q: "How do I format HTML online?"
    a: "Paste the HTML into the box and press Format, or Ctrl+Enter. Choose the indent, print width and whitespace sensitivity first if you need a particular style."
  - q: "Why did the formatter not put every tag on its own line?"
    a: "Inline elements like span, a and strong are whitespace-sensitive: a line break between them can show up as a visible space. With the default css sensitivity, Prettier only breaks lines where it cannot change how the page looks. Choose ignore if you do not mind small spacing changes."
  - q: "Does it format CSS and JavaScript inside HTML?"
    a: "Yes. The contents of style tags are formatted as CSS and the contents of script tags as JavaScript. If that code has a syntax error, it is left as it was."
  - q: "What does 'Unexpected closing tag' mean?"
    a: "The parser found a closing tag with no matching open element, usually because an element was closed in the wrong order or a block element such as a div was placed inside a p, which closes the p early. The error gives the line and column."
  - q: "Can I minify HTML here?"
    a: "Yes, choose Minify. It removes comments and the whitespace between tags, keeping one space between inline elements and leaving pre, textarea, script and style untouched. It does not compress inline CSS or JavaScript."
  - q: "Is 2 or 4 spaces better for HTML?"
    a: "Both are common. 2 spaces is the most widely used for HTML because markup nests deeply; Google's HTML style guide uses 2 spaces. Pick one and use it consistently."
  - q: "Is my HTML uploaded?"
    a: "No. The formatter is downloaded from this site and runs in your browser. Your code is not sent, stored or added to the page address; only your option choices are remembered on this device."
sources:
  - name: "WHATWG HTML Living Standard: Optional tags"
    url: "https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/syntax.html#optional-tags"
  - name: "WHATWG HTML Living Standard: The p element (content model)"
