    <section class="content-section" aria-labelledby="conventions-heading">
      <h2 id="conventions-heading">HTML formatting conventions</h2>
      <p>HTML has no single official style guide, but most teams and style guides, including Google's HTML/CSS style guide, agree on a few habits that make markup easy to read and review:</p>
      <ul class="content-list">
        <li><strong>One block element per line,</strong> with its children indented one level. Short elements whose content fits on the line, such as <code>&lt;li&gt;Notebook&lt;/li&gt;</code>, stay on one line.</li>
        <li><strong>Consistent indentation,</strong> usually 2 spaces because HTML nests deeply. Never mix tabs and spaces in one file.</li>
        <li><strong>Lowercase element and attribute names</strong> and double-quoted attribute values, such as <code>&lt;a href="/contact/"&gt;</code>.</li>
        <li><strong>Long tags wrapped one attribute per line</strong> when they go past the line width, with the closing <code>&gt;</code> on its own line.</li>
        <li><strong>Embedded CSS and JavaScript</strong> indented inside their <code>&lt;style&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> tags and formatted like any other CSS or JavaScript file.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>The formatter changes layout only. It does not rename classes, reorder attributes or fix invalid nesting, and the page looks the same afterwards, except where you choose the ignore whitespace setting.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section" aria-labelledby="whitespace-heading">
      <h2 id="whitespace-heading">Why inline elements are whitespace-sensitive</h2>
      <p>Browsers collapse runs of spaces, tabs and line breaks in text into a single space, but they do not remove that space entirely. So <code>&lt;b&gt;Price:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;span&gt;$5&lt;/span&gt;</code> displays as "Price:$5", while the same two elements on separate lines display as "Price: $5". For block elements such as <code>&lt;div&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;p&gt;</code> the space is invisible, so adding line breaks around them is always safe.</p>
      <p>Prettier's <strong>css</strong> setting uses each element's default CSS display value to decide where breaks are safe. That is why you will sometimes see a closing <code>&gt;</code> moved to the start of the next line: it lets the line break sit inside the tag, where it cannot become a space. <strong>strict</strong> treats every element as inline, which is safest for pages that change display with CSS. <strong>ignore</strong> formats freely and is fine for markup where small spacing changes do not matter.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section" aria-labelledby="minify-heading">
      <h2 id="minify-heading">What minify does, and what it does not</h2>
      <p>Minify mode is deliberately conservative so it never changes how a page looks:</p>
      <ul class="content-list">
        <li>It removes HTML comments, except conditional comments such as <code>&lt;!--[if IE]&gt;</code>.</li>
        <li>It removes whitespace next to block elements and collapses whitespace between inline elements and text to one space.</li>
        <li>It leaves <code>&lt;pre&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;textarea&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;style&gt;</code> exactly as written.</li>
        <li>It does not minify inline CSS or JavaScript, remove optional tags or attribute quotes, or shorten URLs. Build tools such as html-minifier-terser do that as part of a production build.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Servers usually compress HTML with gzip or Brotli anyway, which removes most of the benefit of whitespace removal. Minifying by hand is most useful for small snippets, such as an email template or embed code.</p>
    </section>

